This version stores message pointers directly in the lookup table instead of indices. A hit is then one load, with no index-to-address multiply and no `0xFFFF` sentinel check. Missing entries are `NULL`.

- **Memory:** the table grows from 2 KB to 8 KB. A decode only looks up a few message numbers, so only a few cache lines are actually touched.
- **Behavior:** this is unchanged. The table is still filled in reverse, so the first matching message wins, as in a linear search. Numbers of 1024 and above still use the linear fallback.

I haven't measured the speedup, and I expect it to be small.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    enum { SMALL = 1024 };
    struct Index
    {
        const Profile::MESG* ptr[SMALL];
        Index()
        {
            for (int k = 0; k < SMALL; k++)
                ptr[k] = NULL;
            for (int i = MESGS - 1; i >= 0; i--)
            {
                const FIT_UINT16 n = (FIT_UINT16)mesgs[i].num;
                if (n < SMALL)
                    ptr[n] = &mesgs[i];
            }
        }
    };
    static const Index table;

    if (num < SMALL)
        return table.ptr[num];

    for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
    {
        if ((FIT_UINT16)mesgs[i].num == num)
            return &mesgs[i];
    }
    return NULL;
}
```